<!--
  a link that leaves the app, set at the end of the link's own text.

  **the arrow leaving the box is the whole of it.** every other link on the About screen opens a screen
  of this app; these hand the tap to a browser, and the mark is what says so before it is pressed
  rather than after.

  **it is a drawable rather than the character U+2197, which is what stood here first.** the app's own
  face does not carry that character, so the platform substituted one from elsewhere: the arrow came
  out a lighter stroke than the word beside it and sat nearer cap height than the text's optical
  centre. a vector is the same shape at the weight and position this app chooses.

  **it is sized for the line it is set on rather than at Material's 24dp.** a compound drawable is
  drawn at its own intrinsic size, so the size is this file's to state, and 24dp beside body text is an
  icon rather than a mark.

  Material's `open_in_new`, at Material Symbols' 960 viewport. it carries no colour: the style that
  places it tints it with the same attribute the text takes, so the two cannot drift.

  **the box carries empty space above the glyph, and that is how it is aligned.** a compound drawable
  is centred on the text view's line box, whose centre sits above the text's own optical centre, so
  left alone the mark rides high. nothing at the point of use can move it, but a taller box does:
  adding E of space above the glyph keeps the box centred and therefore moves the glyph down by E/2.
  so the space above is twice the shift wanted, 3.4dp here, measured against the line this mark
  follows rather than guessed.

  **it scales with the text and is not a constant.** the shift comes out of the font's metrics, so a
  different text size wants it measured again rather than copied. `ratio.py` and `align.py` beside this
  project's notes are what measure it.
-->
